# Turn Resolution Budget

- **Hard usability limit:** End-to-end next-turn resolution (from user confirm to terminal result ready for UI apply) must complete in **<= 10,000 ms** on the project target environment. Treat this as a release-blocking requirement, not a best-effort goal.
- **No functional drift:** Performance work must preserve turn-resolution semantics and accepted-order contracts defined by `SPEC/program/turn-resolution.md`, `SPEC/program/turn-resolution-phases.md`, and `SPEC/program/order-suggestions.md`.
- **Budget overflows are defects:** Any change or baseline showing sustained turn-resolution latency above 10 seconds must be treated as a bug and fixed before merge.

## Required profiling signals

- Keep timing visibility for worker and app runner boundaries (`session_start`, `session_complete`, `decode_complete`, `ai_complete`, `resolve_complete`, `success_ready`) per `SPEC/program/logging/turn-resolution.md`.
- When investigating regressions, report phase-level timing slices (AI planning, merge, resolver phases, decode/apply) and payload size indicators.

## Required optimization approach when over budget

- **Bound candidate search:** Apply deterministic probe/acceptance caps to high-cardinality suggestion loops where full enumeration is not required by user-facing behavior.
- **Reuse validation state:** Prefer incremental candidate validation and per-pass caches over repeated full-pass validation setup.
- **Avoid repeated expensive scoring:** Memoize per-target/per-player computations inside hot loops when outcomes are deterministic for the same input set.
- **Control logging overhead:** Keep high-frequency loops summary-only; avoid per-candidate debug logs in tight paths.
- **Preserve determinism:** Any optimization (caps, memoization, caching) must keep deterministic ordering/selection behavior for fixed inputs.
